<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waleran III, Duke of Limburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1226">1226</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Waleran III (d. 1226) was Duke of Limburg from 1221, a son of Duke Henry III of Limburg. He was active in the struggles between the Welf and Staufen claimants to the German throne. In 1214 he married Ermesinde, heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>; their son Henry founded the Luxembourg line, while Limburg passed to his son by his first marriage, Henry IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/waleran-iii-duke-of-limburg.jpg" alt="Waleran III, Duke of Limburg" width="198" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Augustin Calmet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waleran_III_of_Limburg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Waleran, also called Walram, was born around 1165, a son of Henry III of Limburg and Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>. As a younger son he did not expect to inherit and was at first Lord of Montjoie. He led an adventurous youth and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> in 1192. His first wife was Cunigunda, a daughter of Frederick I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, with whom he had Sophie, who married Frederick of Isenberg, Matilda, who married William III, Count of Jülich, the future Duke Henry IV and a younger Waleran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He supported the imperial candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, but after Philip's death in 1208 he went over to his rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Otto of Brunswick</a>. In 1212 he went with his first cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-duke-of-brabant">Henry I, Duke of Brabant</a>, to Liège, then at war with Guelders. Cunigunda died in 1214, and in May of that year he married Ermesinde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, becoming by right of his wife Count of Luxembourg and Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a>. Their children were Catherine, who married Matthias II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, Henry, later Count of Luxembourg, and Gerhard, Count of Durbuy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He inherited Limburg on his father's death in 1221. Two years later he tried once more to take Namur from the Margrave Philip II, failed, and made peace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> on 13 February 1223. He then attended several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diets</a> and went with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. On his way back he died at Rolduc on 2 July 1226.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waleran III of Limburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its dukes, among them <b>Waleran III</b>, intermarried with Luxembourg and the Rhineland nobility.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-limburg">Duchy of Limburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Rolduc, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.87,6.08&amp;year=1216" aria-label="Rolduc on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4688 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Waleran III, Duke of Limburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5138 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5138 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.87,6.08&amp;year=1216">What was here in 1216? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
